#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167021 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "550bb33b-c427-5667-977d-56ff8c6b458e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Displacement of a Particle as a Function of Time"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2944a0c-49e4-504f-814b-58660cce1be4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = 2\\ln (1 + t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163545} {:answer/id #uuid "cab5b877-64cd-5318-8761-44d2a864729e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = \\frac{1}{2}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163546} {:answer/id #uuid "e99dc0fd-cb7f-580d-a040-9b89cb077f10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = \\ln (1 + t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163547} {:answer/id #uuid "3e4a0fbe-fbb0-5ed6-8577-c75c2555a5c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = 1 - e^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163548} {:answer/id #uuid "3fe0a7ef-2008-5e92-825c-5f5566c56429" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = e^{t} - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163549}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3fe0a7ef-2008-5e92-825c-5f5566c56429" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = e^{t} - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163549} :answer-field/id #uuid "d106f175-8f9b-5a2d-a640-2633c9b956c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163544}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8488fc53-7024-5b0e-acc8-a7217b9f9e03" :question/math-academy-id "q-122170" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, the velocity is given by $v(x)=(1+x)\\,m/s.$ Initially, $P$ is at the origin $O.$ Find $x$ as a function of time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070816/assets/a8a6f49ead700bee856b1bbdbb10848eac0a57609da14e3967916a36eb56f048.png)\n\nWe're given that the velocity function $v(x)$ is\n\n$$\nv(x)=1+x.\n$$\n\nWe know that $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=1+x.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1+x \\\\ \\frac{1}{1+x}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1+x}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1+x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|1+x| & =t+K \\\\ 1+x & =C{e}^{t} \\\\ x & =C{e}^{t}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=0$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=1,$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={e}^{t}-1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317649 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "550bb33b-c427-5667-977d-56ff8c6b458e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Displacement of a Particle as a Function of Time"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "669596c6-f9a3-5496-bd8e-b3e459f53ff5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\sqrt[3]{(t+1)^{2}}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317651} {:answer/id #uuid "ac6a5638-dced-58ba-a259-33d911b80871" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\sqrt[3]{3t-1}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317652} {:answer/id #uuid "7616498e-cebd-5be4-8011-3124669d18a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=2\\sqrt[3]{3t+1}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317653} {:answer/id #uuid "313a6fb7-42fc-56cb-b0ab-d0efca5dd779" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\sqrt[3]{3t+1}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317654} {:answer/id #uuid "c5c37b3b-f70a-5ea2-b27a-33dfc1bbe80f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\sqrt[3]{1-t}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317655}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "313a6fb7-42fc-56cb-b0ab-d0efca5dd779" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\sqrt[3]{3t+1}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317654} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a5db30e-a0e2-5851-8372-5c6ca4a6821c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317650}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9bb25288-c9b6-561e-ae4a-285d8dc9e994" :question/math-academy-id "q-122316" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$ where $x\\ge 0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, the velocity is given by $v(x)=\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}}\\,m/s.$ Initially, $P$ is at the origin $O.$ Find $x$ as a function of time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070816/assets/2093298b6cacb0b29f874d401ccca40560517ff5887763e9a2b88bf4bc051abb.png)\n\nWe're given that the velocity function $v(x)$ is\n\n$$\nv(x)=\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe know that $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}} \\\\ (1+x)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1 \\\\ \\int (1+x)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int (1+x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}(1+x)^{3} & =t+K \\\\ (1+x)^{3} & =3t+C \\\\ 1+x & =\\sqrt[3]{3t+C} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt[3]{3t+C}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=0$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=1,$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\sqrt[3]{3t+1}-1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317670 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1320d414-11eb-58a9-b59f-a6f760e87578" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Differential Equation That Relates the Velocity and Displacement of a Particle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6677d18d-461d-5774-bf35-a28cc70e9477" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=\\sqrt{2({x}^{2}+x+6)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317672} {:answer/id #uuid "ffe2f99b-40f5-5e51-9bc6-887240cd1389" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)={x}^{2}+x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317673} {:answer/id #uuid "ecbeae64-3753-5847-94fc-fb505e5ba435" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+x+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317674} {:answer/id #uuid "86636676-279b-543b-a4c5-5708abc7bd97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=\\frac{4}{3}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+2x+6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317675} {:answer/id #uuid "dc864baf-94b1-5d3a-9a55-8f3568d4f3f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)={x}^{2}+3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317676}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6677d18d-461d-5774-bf35-a28cc70e9477" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=\\sqrt{2({x}^{2}+x+6)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317672} :answer-field/id #uuid "27a372b2-40cb-51a3-9ac4-e34e924cc3d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317671}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "462ad3ee-a76f-525b-b893-9e4b76726600" :question/math-academy-id "q-122338" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,m,$ its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is $(2x+1)\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ If $P$ has a displacement of $1\\,m$ when its velocity is $4\\,m/s,$ find $v$ in terms of $x.$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070816/assets/983beaf02582c5ebaee129e28bba657eb2db5e4da7f74eb4b8eba73b9083a259.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $(2x+1)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=2x+1.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=2x+1.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int (2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}={x}^{2}+x+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $x=1\\,\\text{m}$ when $v=4\\,\\text{m/s},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+x+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(4)^{2} & ={1}^{2}+1+C \\\\ 8 & =2+C \\\\ C & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+x+6 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =2({x}^{2}+x+6) \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{2({x}^{2}+x+6)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the particle is moving in the direction of increasing  $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=\\sqrt{2({x}^{2}+x+6)}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317684 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1320d414-11eb-58a9-b59f-a6f760e87578" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Differential Equation That Relates the Velocity and Displacement of a Particle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f25431ad-c934-5706-bfd0-125d8dba8f3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=-\\sqrt{2(3x+5{x}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317686} {:answer/id #uuid "bb44f4d9-5072-591e-ba32-ab92138a6bab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=-(2+x+{x}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317687} {:answer/id #uuid "b96918df-5265-54d6-9385-017cf6ff4704" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=-2\\sqrt{3x+{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317688} {:answer/id #uuid "3e0d9fd1-76cc-5606-9381-3d41b0ed4fc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=-2\\sqrt{2(x+{x}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317689} {:answer/id #uuid "08c6510b-d1c2-5f66-821c-559b0458025e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=-2(x+{x}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317690}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e0d9fd1-76cc-5606-9381-3d41b0ed4fc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=-2\\sqrt{2(x+{x}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317689} :answer-field/id #uuid "8dc1eca9-9de0-57d7-bc63-4a4743a75b6d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317685}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ab95451c-4d9a-5333-846f-3dc7dd4ef9b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-122340" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of decreasing $x$ where $x>0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,m,$ its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is $4(1+2x)\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ If $P$ has a displacement of $1\\,m$ when its velocity is $-4\\,m/s,$ find $v$ in terms of $x.$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070816/assets/466c0aa389beb6d1a42092b73bae75508ef2c50f1f08cca5efaac20d25615f30.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $4(1+2x)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=4(1+2x).\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=4(1+2x).\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int 4(1+2x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=4(x+{x}^{2})+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $x=1\\,\\text{m}$ when $v=-4\\,\\text{m/s},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =4(x+{x}^{2})+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(-4)^{2} & =4(1+{1}^{2})+C \\\\ 8 & =8+C \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =4(x+{x}^{2})+0 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =8(x+{x}^{2}) \\\\ v & =\\pm 2\\sqrt{2(x+{x}^{2})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)<0,$ so we can discard the positive square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=-2\\sqrt{2(x+{x}^{2})}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368841 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1320d414-11eb-58a9-b59f-a6f760e87578" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Differential Equation That Relates the Velocity and Displacement of a Particle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f9ada89-980c-5636-83a3-b984960f1858" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.9\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368843} {:answer/id #uuid "d6b5c0df-ae0d-561c-9879-2af4bc986d8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.1\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368844} {:answer/id #uuid "95ad5cf5-d9d8-5c17-9688-239210dcd95b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.8\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368845} {:answer/id #uuid "795c6e1f-3402-5d36-8798-32740c0b1689" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.5\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368846} {:answer/id #uuid "f054c795-fd0a-5178-87ba-16d97fcbbf18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.3\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368847}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f9ada89-980c-5636-83a3-b984960f1858" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.9\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368843} :answer-field/id #uuid "14e1cda8-c907-5e47-829e-e1a789c7dcd6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368842}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c26a753a-70e5-5794-8f64-38b83165524c" :question/math-academy-id "q-122346" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x,$ away from a fixed origin $O.$ When the displacement of $P$ from $O$ is $x\\,m,$ its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is $(2+\\frac{x}{2}){m/s}^{2}.$ If $v=5\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,\\text{m},$ find the velocity of $P$ when $x=2\\,\\text{m}.$ Round your answer to one decimal place." :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070816/assets/d9482b116bad201dec2c71f173bf18d7dc2f170acbb37052151bba38a7898450.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $(2+\\frac{x}{2}){m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=2+\\frac{x}{2}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=2+\\frac{x}{2}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x & =\\int (2+\\frac{x}{2})\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=2x+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=5\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,\\text{m},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(5)^{2} & =2(0)+\\frac{{0}^{2}}{4}+C \\\\ \\frac{25}{2} & =0+0+C \\\\ C & =\\frac{25}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =2x+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{25}{2} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+4x+25 \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+4x+25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv=\\sqrt{\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+4x+25}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity of $P$ at $x=2$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2}+4(2)+25} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2+8+25} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{35} \\\\  & ≈5.9\\,\\text{m/s},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."}]}
